---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Button from '../components/Button.astro';
import CardGroup from '../components/CardGroup.astro';
import ActivityCard from '../components/ActivityCard.astro';
import StepList from '../components/StepList.astro';
import Step from '../components/Step.astro';
import IllustrativeExample from '../components/IllustrativeExample.astro';
import Card from '../components/Card.astro';

// Copy: rewritten from content-source/waypoint-content-v1.md, section "2 Discover Waypoint".
// Wes's WEBSITE COPY is the framework; every fact here comes from it.
// Order follows his build notes: activities, the day, support, learning, people, place.
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About Waypoint"
  description="Waypoint is a small, part-time alternative provision being developed in Shoreham-by-Sea, where young people aged 8–16 learn through practical projects."
>
  <PageHero
    title="About Waypoint"
    strapline="Small-group, part-time alternative provision in Shoreham-by-Sea for ages 8–16."
  />

  <Section
    id="what-it-is"
    title="What Waypoint is"
    strapline="Learning by doing, in a small group."
    drawing="building"
    drawingLabel="Line drawing of two stick people carrying a plank, with a toolbox"
  >
    <Fragment slot="intro">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Waypoint is a small, part-time provision being developed in Shoreham-by-Sea. Young people aged 8–16 learn
          through practical projects, with adults who take the time to get to know them.
        </p>
        <p>
          Places come either through a school or, for home-educated young people, through Discovery. Either way, we
          look at the young person, the group and the support available before offering a place.
        </p>
        <p>
          We'll share the confirmed venue, facilities and arrival arrangements before any placement starts, and visits
          can be arranged in advance.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Fragment>
  </Section>

  <Section id="activities" title="Activities" strapline="Building, testing and making things together.">
    <Fragment slot="intro">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Here are four examples from our activity bank. They are not a fixed timetable. We choose activities to suit
          the group, their interests and any individual goals, and the first term is all land-based.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Fragment>
    <CardGroup columns={2}>
      <ActivityCard
        title="Build the Basecamp"
        drawing="basecamp"
        drawingLabel="Line drawing of a tent with a flag between two pine trees"
      >
        Plan and build a temporary base with tarpaulins and ropes. Work out what makes it useful, test ideas and
        improve the design, measuring, explaining choices and sharing the jobs along the way.
      </ActivityCard>
      <ActivityCard
        title="The Giant Chain Reaction"
        drawing="chain-reaction"
        drawingLabel="Line drawing of a ball rolling down a ramp into a row of dominoes"
      >
        Link ramps, balls, tubes and everyday bits into one long sequence. See where it stops, work out why and try
        another way. Listening and solving problems matter as much as the result.
      </ActivityCard>
      <ActivityCard
        title="Survival Village"
        drawing="survival-village"
        drawingLabel="Line drawing of a small village of huts, a tipi with a flag and a campfire"
      >
        Invent a settlement, plan the shelters and decide how to use limited materials. The group has to agree what
        matters most, split the jobs and change the plan when it needs to.
      </ActivityCard>
      <ActivityCard
        title="Waypoint Game Studio"
        drawing="game-studio"
        drawingLabel="Line drawing of two stick people pointing at a screen showing a simple game"
      >
        Come up with characters, rules and levels for a game. The young people lead the ideas, and an adult uses
        digital tools to turn them into a simple game the group can test and improve.
      </ActivityCard>
    </CardGroup>
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="reporting"
    title="Reporting"
    strapline="Real evidence every week, not just “they had a good day”."
    drawing="report"
    drawingLabel="Line drawing of a report sheet with ticks and a pencil"
    drawingSide="left"
  >
    <Fragment slot="intro">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          This is what makes Waypoint different. Every school-funded place comes with a weekly written report for the
          school and family. It shows what the young person actually did, the help they had and what to try next.
        </p>
        <p>
          Schools get real evidence for their next decision, and families hear what their child has been up to,
          including the things worth celebrating.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Fragment>
    <CardGroup columns={3}>
      <Card title="What they did" icon="clipboard">
        The activity, and what the young person actually did or said.
      </Card>
      <Card title="The help they had" icon="heart">
        The support that was planned, and the support that was actually given.
      </Card>
      <Card title="How they got on" icon="star">
        How they responded, including things worth celebrating, like an idea they shared or something they built.
      </Card>
      <Card title="What's still tricky" icon="mountain">
        Anything they still found hard, written down honestly rather than glossed over.
      </Card>
      <Card title="Their own view" icon="speech">
        What the young person thought of their day, included wherever we can.
      </Card>
      <Card title="What to try next" icon="arrow">
        One or two realistic next steps at Waypoint, and ideas the school might want to try.
      </Card>
    </CardGroup>
    <div class="example">
      <IllustrativeExample title="Inside a weekly update" label="Fictional example, Alex is not a real pupil">
        <p>A short extract from one made-up Tuesday, to show the kind of detail a report gives.</p>
        <ul class="update">
          <li><strong>Agreed focus:</strong> Share an idea in a group task, and ask for help when needed.</li>
          <li>
            <strong>What happened:</strong> In a Giant Chain Reaction, Alex suggested changing the slope of a ramp after
            the ball stopped. After an adult showed how to support the ramp and broke the job into steps, Alex helped
            move it and tested the run again.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Help and limits:</strong> Alex joined in with a demonstration and step-by-step help from an adult.
            We didn't see Alex do it alone, and Alex didn't ask for help this time, so we can't say there's progress on
            that part yet.
          </li>
          <li><strong>Alex's view:</strong> Alex said testing the run was the best bit.</li>
          <li>
            <strong>Next at Waypoint:</strong> Keep the demonstration and small steps, and give Alex another chance to
            share an idea or ask for help.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>For the SENCo:</strong> A quick demonstration and the option to sketch an idea might be worth trying
            in school. We haven't shown it will work in class, or that support can be reduced.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </IllustrativeExample>
    </div>
    <div class="prose reviews">
      <p>
        Reports build up over the placement. There is a check-in after the first two sessions, then fuller reviews at
        around weeks 4, 8 and 12. At each one, the school and Waypoint look at the evidence and the young person's
        experience, and decide whether to carry on, change the support or plan the next step.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="button-row">
      <Button href="/for-schools/">How reporting works for schools</Button>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="typical-day"
    title="A typical day"
    strapline="The same shape every Tuesday."
    drawing="day"
    drawingLabel="Line drawing of the sun's path across the sky, with five stops along the way"
  >
    <Fragment slot="intro">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Every day follows the same pattern, so young people know what's coming. We plan to share a simple outline
          in advance, and the activities can still change to suit the group and the conditions on the day.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Fragment>
    <div class="day-steps">
      <StepList>
        <Step title="Arrive and settle">
          <p>A welcome, time to catch up with others and an explanation of the plan for the day.</p>
        </Step>
        <Step title="Get started">
          <p>Start an activity with a demonstration or a small first step.</p>
        </Step>
        <Step title="Breaks and lunch">
          <p>Time to rest, eat and chat.</p>
        </Step>
        <Step title="Carry on with the project">
          <p>Keep going, improve an idea or try something new.</p>
        </Step>
        <Step title="Finish together">
          <p>Look back at something from the day and get ready to go home.</p>
        </Step>
      </StepList>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="our-approach"
    title="Our approach"
    strapline="Help to join in, and to keep going."
    drawing="helping"
    drawingLabel="Line drawing of an adult showing a young person how to stack blocks"
    drawingSide="left"
  >
    <Fragment slot="intro">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Some young people understand a task best when they see it shown. Others need one step at a time, an adult
          alongside them or a short break.
        </p>
        <p>
          We look at what's getting in the way and help them join in, or come back to the activity, with clear
          boundaries and supervision throughout. The aim is a challenge they can manage, and a bit more
          responsibility over time.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Fragment>
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="learning"
    title="Learning"
    strapline="What young people get out of it."
    drawing="steps"
    drawingLabel="Line drawing of a winding route up to a flag"
  >
    <Fragment slot="intro">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Practical projects mean reading instructions, measuring, explaining ideas, agreeing with others and solving
          problems. We record what a young person actually did, and whether they had help, because doing something
          with support and doing it alone tell us different things.
        </p>
        <p>
          For school-funded places, individual goals link what happens on the day to the weekly report. For Discovery,
          the focus is on enjoying practical projects alongside other home-educated young people.
        </p>
        <p>
          Progress isn't always a straight line. Coming up with an idea, trying a new skill, working with someone new
          or asking for help all count.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Fragment>
    <div class="example">
      <IllustrativeExample title="How a project becomes learning" label="Illustrative example, not a real young person">
        <p>
          This shows how one project could work for one young person. It isn't a fixed programme or a real outcome.
        </p>
        <StepList>
          <Step title="Starting point">
            <p>
              A young person who loves making things but finds it hard to share an idea in a group. We start by asking
              about their experience and what helps.
            </p>
          </Step>
          <Step title="Goal">
            <p>Agree a way for them to get an idea across, by speaking, sketching, showing or another way that suits them.</p>
          </Step>
          <Step title="Activity">
            <p>A Giant Chain Reaction, where the group has to work out how to get a ball from one part of the course to the next.</p>
          </Step>
          <Step title="Support">
            <p>An adult might show how a connection works, break the task into smaller steps or help them show their design.</p>
          </Step>
          <Step title="What we notice">
            <p>
              What they actually contributed, the help they used and what they thought of it. A good group result alone
              doesn't show what each person learned.
            </p>
          </Step>
          <Step title="Next step">
            <p>
              Come back to a similar chance in a later session, keep the support that helped and see what happens. Next
              time that might mean explaining a change or trying the idea in a different project.
            </p>
          </Step>
        </StepList>
      </IllustrativeExample>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="good-fit"
    title="Is Waypoint a good fit?"
    strapline="Talk to us first."
    brand
    drawing="signpost"
    drawingLabel="Line drawing of a stick person looking up at a signpost"
    drawingSide="left"
  >
    <Fragment slot="intro">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          We talk through each young person's strengths, needs and circumstances before offering a place. Find the
          information for your situation, or ask us a question.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ul class="route-links">
        <li><a href="/for-schools/">For schools</a></li>
        <li><a href="/parents-and-carers/">Parents and carers</a></li>
        <li><a href="/home-education/">Home education</a></li>
        <li><a href="/for-young-people/">For young people</a></li>
      </ul>
      <div class="button-row">
        <Button href="/contact/" variant="accent">Get in touch</Button>
      </div>
    </Fragment>
  </Section>

</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .day-steps,
  .example {
    margin-top: 2rem;
    max-width: 46rem;
  }

  .example :global(.step-list) {
    margin: 1.25rem 0;
  }

  .update {
    margin: 1rem 0 0;
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
  }

  .update li + li {
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
  }

  .reviews {
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  .route-links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem 1.5rem;
    margin: 1rem 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .route-links a::after {
    content: ' \2192';
  }
</style>
